{# The HTML alternative. Its plain-text twin, `verify_email.txt.twig`, is the mandatory one — see
   the comment at the top of that file and `TwigVerificationMailer`.

   IT DOES NOT EXTEND `base.html.twig`, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT. The layout is a *web page*: it
   emits an importmap, a favicon data-URI, a flash-message region and a hot-reload script. None of
   that means anything in an inbox, and half of it is actively harmful there — an external `<script>`
   is a spam signal and will be stripped, and `app.request` does not exist when a Messenger worker
   renders this. An email is a different medium that happens to share a syntax.

   Email HTML is also a decade behind web HTML on purpose: Gmail strips `<head>`, so styling is
   inline; Outlook renders through Word's engine, so layout is tables when it is anything. This
   message is a heading, three paragraphs and a link, so it needs none of that machinery — the
   simplest markup that renders identically everywhere is the correct markup, and the first slice
   that needs a real email design can introduce a proper mail layout template then.

   No images, no tracking pixel, no web fonts, no CSS from anywhere. A message that fetches nothing
   renders the same with remote content blocked (which is the default in most clients), and it has
   nothing to leak the recipient's reading habits with. #}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
    <head>
        <meta charset="UTF-8">
        <title>Confirm your email address</title>
    </head>
    <body style="font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.5; color: #222;">
        <h1 style="font-size: 20px;">Confirm your email address</h1>

        {# The only address rendered anywhere in this message, and it is the recipient's own
           (AC-4, AC-32). Twig autoescapes it, which matters more here than on a normal page: an
           address is user-supplied and this is the one piece of dynamic text in the file. #}
        <p>Somebody &mdash; we hope you &mdash; created a muzbar account with this address:
            <strong>{{ recipientEmail }}</strong>.</p>

        <p>Open the link below to confirm the address and finish setting the account up:</p>

        {# The full URL is the visible link text, not "click here". Three reasons, all practical: a
           recipient can see where the link goes before following it, which is precisely the habit
           phishing relies on people not having; a client that strips the anchor still leaves
           something copy-pasteable; and a link whose text and href differ is a spam-filter signal.
           It is long and slightly ugly. That is a fair price. #}
        <p><a href="{{ verificationUrl }}">{{ verificationUrl }}</a></p>

        <p>The link works once and expires in {{ lifetimeHours }} hours, at
            {# Timezone pinned explicitly — see the identical note in the text template for why the
               filter's default would eventually make the word "UTC" a lie. #}
            {{ expiresAt|date('Y-m-d H:i', 'UTC') }} UTC.
            If it has already expired you can ask for a new one at any time.</p>

        <p>If you did not create this account you do not need to do anything. Without the link above
            the account cannot be used to sign in, and it will simply sit there unverified.</p>

        <hr>

        <p style="color: #666; font-size: 13px;">This is an automated message from muzbar. Replies to
            it are not read.</p>
    </body>
</html>
